(4439727, 635671, 63503, '<td valign="top">Such a beautiful image. Well deserved ribbon. Congratulations Oliver.</td>', 'Such a beautiful image. Well deserved ribbon. Congratulations Oliver.', '2008-01-30 11:38:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(4439790, 635532, 40718, '<td valign="top">Technicals:  I think you did well here.  It''s almost a 6 which almost always indicates that the technicals were well done.  You chose a nice perspective to show off both the wording and give depth to the engine.  The lighting is well done as well as the B&amp;W conversion.  One issue, which you cannot help, is the distortion the 10-22 lens gave you.  I can see it mainly at the lower right.  Again, though, what can you do about that?  The whites on the front stripes are just a bit overdone and I may have been tempted to try to reveal a bit more in the central shadows as the center of the picture is pretty dark (thus not giving us something to look at except on the periphery of the canvas).<br/> <br/>The feel: Feels like a nice ad.</td>', 'Technicals:  I think you did well here.  It''s almost a 6 which almost always indicates that the technicals were well done.  You chose a nice perspective to show off both the wording and give depth to the engine.  The lighting is well done as well as the B&W conversion.  One issue, which you cannot help, is the distortion the 10-22 lens gave you.  I can see it mainly at the lower right.  Again, though, what can you do about that?  The whites on the front stripes are just a bit overdone and I may have been tempted to try to reveal a bit more in the central shadows as the center of the picture is pretty dark (thus not giving us something to look at except on the periphery of the canvas). The feel: Feels like a nice ad.', '2008-01-30 11:55:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(4440194, 635671, 67855, '<td valign="top">Good exposure, good tones &amp; colors, a ribbon well deserved<br/>:)</td>', 'Good exposure, good tones & colors, a ribbon well deserved:)', '2008-01-30 13:44:52.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(4440278, 636079, 81792, '<td valign="top">Congratulations Steve - great shot - well deserved ribbon.</td>', 'Congratulations Steve - great shot - well deserved ribbon.', '2008-01-30 14:11:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(4440663, 634828, 65083, '<td valign="top">wow congrats on the ribbon.</td>', 'wow congrats on the ribbon.', '2008-01-30 15:35:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(4440664, 636067, 67697, '<td valign="top">Now this one I DO remember because I liked the simplicity of the shot.  I love the black and white tones and the details on the shells.  This is the kind of photo that would look pretty printed on antique paper.</td>', 'Now this one I DO remember because I liked the simplicity of the shot.  I love the black and white tones and the details on the shells.  This is the kind of photo that would look pretty printed on antique paper.', '2008-01-30 15:35:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(4441765, 636079, 66868, '<td valign="top">Hi Steve,<br/> <br/>Nice to see you keeping up the shots.  Great shot, well deserved ribbon--congratulations.</td>', 'Hi Steve, Nice to see you keeping up the shots.  Great shot, well deserved ribbon--congratulations.', '2008-01-30 20:57:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443195, 634883, 66597, '<td valign="top">An interesting concept and creative take on the challenge. I''ll confess my eye is drawn more to the very bright areas behind the main subject clothes pin than the subject itself, though. This is particularly troublesome on the left-hand side of the photo where there are no interesting shadows crisscrossing the large blown area. I appreciate, however, that this may be a stylistic choice on your part, and again I do find this very creative.</td>', 'An interesting concept and creative take on the challenge. I''ll confess my eye is drawn more to the very bright areas behind the main subject clothes pin than the subject itself, though. This is particularly troublesome on the left-hand side of the photo where there are no interesting shadows crisscrossing the large blown area. I appreciate, however, that this may be a stylistic choice on your part, and again I do find this very creative.', '2008-01-31 11:12:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443202, 636061, 66597, '<td valign="top">I confess I''m not sure what you were going for in this shot, Jayson. I''ve seen a lot of your work that is beautiful and artistic, and this one doesn''t quite hit the mark for me - sorry :-( I would, however, like to know what these mounds are, so the photo generates some curiosity. And you do have some nice textures going.</td>', 'I confess I''m not sure what you were going for in this shot, Jayson. I''ve seen a lot of your work that is beautiful and artistic, and this one doesn''t quite hit the mark for me - sorry :-( I would, however, like to know what these mounds are, so the photo generates some curiosity. And you do have some nice textures going.', '2008-01-31 11:16:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443213, 635772, 66597, '<td valign="top">This is an interesting take on the challenge, Don. It is certainly open for interpretation, which I like. I''m not going to comment on the technical aspects, because this photo is appears to be about a message, not technique. I do think the strongest aspect is the lighting behind the windows and the two humps that are probably fence posts but could be headstones - which certainly would reinforce your tribute to our most heinous economic conditions. Anyway, bottom line - I like this. It''s interesting if not completely captivating.</td>', 'This is an interesting take on the challenge, Don. It is certainly open for interpretation, which I like. I''m not going to comment on the technical aspects, because this photo is appears to be about a message, not technique. I do think the strongest aspect is the lighting behind the windows and the two humps that are probably fence posts but could be headstones - which certainly would reinforce your tribute to our most heinous economic conditions. Anyway, bottom line - I like this. It''s interesting if not completely captivating.', '2008-01-31 11:19:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443225, 636232, 66597, '<td valign="top">This is a lovely b/w conversion, Jeffrey. I didn''t even know you were entering it, but still didn''t vote, as I didn''t get around to voting this challenge. LOL. Anyway - lots of stuff to like in this photo - excellent lighting with emphasis on the candles and not the face, a sense of mystery, a message. The one thing that doesn''t quite work for me is the composition. The candles almost form a horizon across the center of the photo which makes it look a bit square to me. But still a lovely photo and most worthy entry!</td>', 'This is a lovely b/w conversion, Jeffrey. I didn''t even know you were entering it, but still didn''t vote, as I didn''t get around to voting this challenge. LOL. Anyway - lots of stuff to like in this photo - excellent lighting with emphasis on the candles and not the face, a sense of mystery, a message. The one thing that doesn''t quite work for me is the composition. The candles almost form a horizon across the center of the photo which makes it look a bit square to me. But still a lovely photo and most worthy entry!', '2008-01-31 11:23:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443229, 635154, 66597, '<td valign="top">This is a very creative entry that I think might have scored a bit higher - certainly your commenters liked it a lot. It''s very sharp, with beautiful lines and nice processing. The only nit is perhaps it could be a bit warmer - I''d love to see the rich tones of the wall brought out a bit more.</td>', 'This is a very creative entry that I think might have scored a bit higher - certainly your commenters liked it a lot. It''s very sharp, with beautiful lines and nice processing. The only nit is perhaps it could be a bit warmer - I''d love to see the rich tones of the wall brought out a bit more.', '2008-01-31 11:25:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(4443790, 635702, 85548, '<td valign="top">I like this image.  Do you have a bullmastiff behind that window?</td>', 'I like this image.  Do you have a bullmastiff behind that window?', '2008-01-31 14:56:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(4444646, 635031, 23002, '<td valign="top">Hello, and greetings from the critique club. :)<br/> <br/>For this image I love the colours.  That gives it immediate interest.  It appears to be a tad on the bright side, and the lighting is fairly harsh, I would guess from a flash.  The focus is excellent, and it certainly fits the challenge.<br/> <br/>If you were to retake this photo, try bouncing the flash instead of using direct flash on the subject, or diffuse the flash.  I think that would make a world of difference on this shot.<br/> <br/>I look forward to seeing more of your florals.<br/> <br/>Ernie</td>', 'Hello, and greetings from the critique club. :) For this image I love the colours.  That gives it immediate interest.  It appears to be a tad on the bright side, and the lighting is fairly harsh, I would guess from a flash.  The focus is excellent, and it certainly fits the challenge. If you were to retake this photo, try bouncing the flash instead of using direct flash on the subject, or diffuse the flash.  I think that would make a world of difference on this shot. I look forward to seeing more of your florals. Ernie', '2008-01-31 21:53:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447338, 635268, 61651, '<td valign="top">Nice catch, Alan.  It''s funny but the 6 is even more pronounced and noticeable when viewed as a thumbnail.</td>', 'Nice catch, Alan.  It''s funny but the 6 is even more pronounced and noticeable when viewed as a thumbnail.', '2008-02-01 15:11:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447362, 635464, 61651, '<td valign="top">Nice defining highlights on the metal rings of that bowl.  Such a simple shot, even simple lighting, but it''s perfect.  Congratulations on 6th place!</td>', 'Nice defining highlights on the metal rings of that bowl.  Such a simple shot, even simple lighting, but it''s perfect.  Congratulations on 6th place!', '2008-02-01 15:15:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447372, 634883, 61651, '<td valign="top">Who said centered-subject compositions are boring!  Love this, and great for the challenge.</td>', 'Who said centered-subject compositions are boring!  Love this, and great for the challenge.', '2008-02-01 15:18:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447392, 635751, 61651, '<td valign="top">Focus on those 33 7+ votes you got - the dpc masses would have shouted louder if you *hadn''t* indicated the six connection in your title, they just don''t know it.  From what I know of these types of long exposures, you appear to have done an excellent job from a technical standpoint.  The traffic blur takes us across the frame quite nicely.</td>', 'Focus on those 33 7+ votes you got - the dpc masses would have shouted louder if you *hadn''t* indicated the six connection in your title, they just don''t know it.  From what I know of these types of long exposures, you appear to have done an excellent job from a technical standpoint.  The traffic blur takes us across the frame quite nicely.', '2008-02-01 15:24:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447397, 635499, 61651, '<td valign="top">Another delightful and colorful shot from you, Shez.  I love GeneralE''s suggestion, you should try it!</td>', 'Another delightful and colorful shot from you, Shez.  I love GeneralE''s suggestion, you should try it!', '2008-02-01 15:26:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447411, 634828, 61651, '<td valign="top">Congratulations, Les!  Lovely color and strong silhouette.</td>', 'Congratulations, Les!  Lovely color and strong silhouette.', '2008-02-01 15:28:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(4447454, 635772, 61651, '<td valign="top">I liked this one a lot and gave it an 8, glad to see it was from a TSer.  The light just does it for me, and I say that knowing full well I''m the type of photographer who likely would have stopped myself from taking such a picture, because of the position of the light.  I''ve got to get over that. :)</td>', 'I liked this one a lot and gave it an 8, glad to see it was from a TSer.  The light just does it for me, and I say that knowing full well I''m the type of photographer who likely would have stopped myself from taking such a picture, because of the position of the light.  I''ve got to get over that. :)', '2008-02-01 15:35:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(4448781, 635572, 62787, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>First reaction: "Oh. A squint picture of a church."<br/> <br/>You''ve met the challenge, you''ve gone for a slightly unusual composition, that''s all good, but there''s something about this image that doesn''t quite work for me. I''ll try and explain why, and I hope you consider it constructive...<br/> <br/>I think partly it''s to do with the subject matter. Churches are already ornate things in themselves, they get photographed a lot, and the picture needs to be pretty damn striking to really get a positive reaction. So just average ones tend to get an extra negative reaction of "Hmm, wow, another church. How exciting.", which the quality of the picture in itself may not entirely justify.<br/> <br/>Composition... I like the positioning of the steeple, but I don''t like the angle of the church, it makes me feel dizzy. And those gravestones poking out of the bottom right of the picture seem a bit out of place. So maybe the first thing to suggest would be to experiment with different amounts of zooming, positions and angles, and try and find one that really works. <br/> <br/>Since the editing rules now allow it, you could also try playing with tonemapping multiple exposures. I know you couldn''t have done it handheld, but if you do decide to go back with a tripod...<br/> <br/>Another problem I had with the image was that I find the light and the colours a bit unappealing. The light is mainly flat, except for those awkward tombstone shadows. And the colour comes across as either too warm (the church) or too cold (the steeple). Might be an idea to tweak with those separately in post-processing, and try and get the colour temperatures to work. <br/> <br/>Maybe some dodging and burning or soft light layers would make the light more interesting, but there''s only so much you can do. Instead, a more fun way to do it might be to do a long exposure and selectively light up bits of the church with a flashlight, or set off your flash manually a couple of times in different places. Just something to experiment with.<br/> <br/>Anyway, sorry, I don''t find this image works for me, but I''d be intrigued to see if you could make it work... So a little personal challenge could be to go back and reshoot the same church, play with it, and see what else you could come up with :)<br/> <br/>Good luck and hope this helps.<br/> <br/>Jelena</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.  First reaction: "Oh. A squint picture of a church." You''ve met the challenge, you''ve gone for a slightly unusual composition, that''s all good, but there''s something about this image that doesn''t quite work for me. I''ll try and explain why, and I hope you consider it constructive... I think partly it''s to do with the subject matter. Churches are already ornate things in themselves, they get photographed a lot, and the picture needs to be pretty damn striking to really get a positive reaction. So just average ones tend to get an extra negative reaction of "Hmm, wow, another church. How exciting.", which the quality of the picture in itself may not entirely justify. Composition... I like the positioning of the steeple, but I don''t like the angle of the church, it makes me feel dizzy. And those gravestones poking out of the bottom right of the picture seem a bit out of place. So maybe the first thing to suggest would be to experiment with different amounts of zooming, positions and angles, and try and find one that really works.  Since the editing rules now allow it, you could also try playing with tonemapping multiple exposures. I know you couldn''t have done it handheld, but if you do decide to go back with a tripod... Another problem I had with the image was that I find the light and the colours a bit unappealing. The light is mainly flat, except for those awkward tombstone shadows. And the colour comes across as either too warm (the church) or too cold (the steeple). Might be an idea to tweak with those separately in post-processing, and try and get the colour temperatures to work.  Maybe some dodging and burning or soft light layers would make the light more interesting, but there''s only so much you can do. Instead, a more fun way to do it might be to do a long exposure and selectively light up bits of the church with a flashlight, or set off your flash manually a couple of times in different places. Just something to experiment with. Anyway, sorry, I don''t find this image works for me, but I''d be intrigued to see if you could make it work... So a little personal challenge could be to go back and reshoot the same church, play with it, and see what else you could come up with :) Good luck and hope this helps. Jelena', '2008-02-01 20:43:23.000000', NULL, 0),
